<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Relations Q10 - What the horizontal line test determines</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>What does the horizontal line test determine for a given function?</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>The <strong>horizontal line test</strong> is used once we know a graph represents a function. Imagine a horizontal line at some height \(y\). Every point where it meets the graph is an input that gives that output.</p>
<p>If every horizontal line meets the graph at most once, each output comes from only one input, so the function is <strong>one-to-one</strong>. A one-to-one function can be reversed without conflict, so it has an inverse function. If some horizontal line meets the graph more than once, the function is many-to-one and has no inverse unless its domain is restricted.</p>
<p>Option B describes the vertical line test, which decides whether a graph is a function at all. Options A and C describe other features of a graph (symmetry and the intercept at the origin), which the horizontal line test does not check. The correct answer is D.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
